{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-reader-brief.md clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r6.json'","aggregated_output":"# The loss trace: was the fact extracted? (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs, and do not read any file other than this brief and the one\npacket you are given.\n\nEach point of your packet holds one **decisive item**: a statement an earlier reader, called reader B, cited for a frozen answer (its day,\ntitle, who said it, the passage and its meaning). Beside it is **every evidence item a system kept from that same article** (each with a\n`ref`, a summary and the passages it quotes); the list is empty when the system kept nothing from the article.\n\nFor each point, answer one question: does one of the kept items **state the decisive item's fact** — the same fact or figure, in any wording?\nName the kept item's `ref` that states it (the first if several do), or `none` when no kept item states it. Search the kept items for the\nfigure or the fact; do not name an item because it is related, from the same article, or about the same topic.\n\nReturn one JSON object per point, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else (no prose, no code fence):\n`{\"point\": 3, \"extracted\": \"<ref>\"|\"none\", \"why\": \"one sentence naming the figure or fact that decides it\"}`.\n[\n {\n  \"point\": 1,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"Micron (as reported by the author)\",\n   \"passage\": \"tight market conditions could last until 2028\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Micron (memory supplier)\",\n    \"what\": \"memory market stays tight: demand above supply, prices sharply higher, new industry capacity not online until 2027 or later\",\n    \"period\": \"until 2028\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology said in its latest quarterly announcement that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com's report; Micron gave no size for the shortage beyond the 2028 date.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury forecast that new memory-industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory and hence low supply for at least the next year and a half, which could push memory chip prices even higher because demand is not slowing.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com reported that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year the figure could be over $1 trillion, which the article says will raise demand for memory chips as well as for NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided revenue for its next quarter to $50 billion, which Fool.com said is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate in the comparable period; Micron gave the $50 billion guidance without specifying NVIDIA's expected figure.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 2,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"the article's author\",\n   \"passage\": \"new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"the article's author\",\n    \"what\": \"new memory capacity comes online only in 2027 or later, so memory supply stays low\",\n    \"period\": \"at least the next year and a half\",\n    \"status\": \"view with a reason\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology said in its latest quarterly announcement that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com's report; Micron gave no size for the shortage beyond the 2028 date.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury forecast that new memory-industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory and hence low supply for at least the next year and a half, which could push memory chip prices even higher because demand is not slowing.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com reported that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year the figure could be over $1 trillion, which the article says will raise demand for memory chips as well as for NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided revenue for its next quarter to $50 billion, which Fool.com said is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate in the comparable period; Micron gave the $50 billion guidance without specifying NVIDIA's expected figure.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 3,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"Micron (guidance, as reported by the author)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Micron\",\n    \"what\": \"revenue guidance\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$50bn\",\n    \"period\": \"Micron's next fiscal quarter\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast (guidance)\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology said in its latest quarterly announcement that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com's report; Micron gave no size for the shortage beyond the 2028 date.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury forecast that new memory-industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory and hence low supply for at least the next year and a half, which could push memory chip prices even higher because demand is not slowing.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com reported that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year the figure could be over $1 trillion, which the article says will raise demand for memory chips as well as for NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided revenue for its next quarter to $50 billion, which Fool.com said is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate in the comparable period; Micron gave the $50 billion guidance without specifying NVIDIA's expected figure.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 4,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n   \"who\": \"Micron (guidance, as cited)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Micron guidance\",\n    \"what\": \"adjusted gross margin (memory pricing power)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"86%\",\n    \"period\": \"next quarter\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across the company's upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems, according to 24/7 Wall St.; the report does not identify the source of the delay reports, name which Vera Rubin products are affected, or give a new timing.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center revenue up 92% to $75.2 billion, according to 24/7 Wall St. (the quarter is not specified in the item).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Broadcom guided fiscal Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year — a competitor's projected AI chip revenue in the market NVIDIA leads.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts a 27% compound annual growth rate for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators, as cited by 24/7 Wall St., with Broadcom's custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta taking share of the AI chip story.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Memory supplier Micron reported Q3 revenue rose 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, with CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointing to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements intended to lock in durability, according to 24/7 Wall St.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron is forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% for the next quarter, above the peak gross margin NVIDIA has ever achieved, 24/7 Wall St. reports — a memory supplier's margin outlook that reflects pricing power in NVIDIA's input chain.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Unnamed estimates cited by 24/7 Wall St. put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-8\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA carries $119 billion in supply commitments, which 24/7 Wall St. says hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools (the item gives no date or breakdown for the commitment).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 5,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   \"title\": \"China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\",\n   \"who\": \"The Information, citing two unidentified people (via Bloomberg / Straits Times)\",\n   \"passage\": \"China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Chinese officials\",\n    \"what\": \"permission for top AI companies (Alibaba, ByteDance, DeepSeek) to buy NVIDIA H200 chips, each on approval stating the number and reason\",\n    \"how_much\": \"a limited amount; total may be fewer than 200,000\",\n    \"condition\": \"approval per request; limited amount\",\n    \"status\": \"rumour\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information reported on July 8, 2026, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter, that China plans to allow top AI companies including Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek to buy a limited amount of NVIDIA H200 AI chips, easing previous restrictions. Chinese officials informed the companies they will have permission to purchase some processors; companies must specify how many chips they need and why to get approval, and the total may end up below 200,000 chips.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site.\",\n     \"Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .\",\n     \"The companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information.\",\n     \"The Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported.\",\n     \"But soaring demand for AI processors helped spur the Chinese authorities to allow some H200 purchases, according to the Information, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress said in May 2026 that the H200 chips had not generated any revenue for NVIDIA in China and that NVIDIA was uncertain whether any imports would be allowed into the country.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"In May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.\",\n     \"“We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA is working to release its more powerful Rubin AI chip line-up in the second half of calendar 2026, the article reports.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Washington has continued to restrict sales to China of more capable AI chips than the H200 on national security grounds, the article reports.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Washington has continued to restrict sales of more capable AI chips to China on national security grounds.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"US President Donald Trump granted NVIDIA permission in December to sell the H200 processors to China, although Chinese authorities had been slow to allow the technology into the country, the article reports.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Though US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell the processors to China in December, the Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 6,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n   \"who\": \"China's government (stated by Tom's Hardware)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Beijing\",\n    \"what\": \"Chinese companies not allowed to buy NVIDIA H200\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has reportedly created a new 'whitelist' of verified customers in Asia, cutting its number of authorized clients by more than half; remaining customers passed tougher compliance inspections — field staff visits to customer data centers, contract verification and end-user interviews — to screen out shell companies that forward NVIDIA GPUs and servers into China. Sources told the Financial Times the move followed pressure from Washington to tighten legal compliance. NVIDIA did not immediately comment.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\",\n     \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Beijing has refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA's U.S.-approved AI processors (H200 GPUs), instead relying on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the compute shortfall — a policy that bars the China sales Washington cleared, with domestic supply 'apparently still not enough.'\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In December 2025, President Donald Trump reversed U.S. policy and allowed NVIDIA to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in China/Asia, a step that would have eased the region's AI-chip shortage.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all Chinese domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out, and Chinese buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use — indicating unserved AI compute demand in China amid constrained supply.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 7,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n   \"who\": \"an unnamed tech executive (to the Financial Times)\",\n   \"passage\": \"all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"unnamed Chinese tech executive\",\n    \"what\": \"China's domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out; buyers are considering less powerful chips\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has reportedly created a new 'whitelist' of verified customers in Asia, cutting its number of authorized clients by more than half; remaining customers passed tougher compliance inspections — field staff visits to customer data centers, contract verification and end-user interviews — to screen out shell companies that forward NVIDIA GPUs and servers into China. Sources told the Financial Times the move followed pressure from Washington to tighten legal compliance. NVIDIA did not immediately comment.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\",\n     \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Beijing has refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA's U.S.-approved AI processors (H200 GPUs), instead relying on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the compute shortfall — a policy that bars the China sales Washington cleared, with domestic supply 'apparently still not enough.'\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In December 2025, President Donald Trump reversed U.S. policy and allowed NVIDIA to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in China/Asia, a step that would have eased the region's AI-chip shortage.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all Chinese domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out, and Chinese buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use — indicating unserved AI compute demand in China amid constrained supply.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 8,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n   \"who\": \"US President (as reported)\",\n   \"passage\": \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"US President Trump\",\n    \"what\": \"allowed H200 exports to select customers in China\",\n    \"period\": \"December 2025\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has reportedly created a new 'whitelist' of verified customers in Asia, cutting its number of authorized clients by more than half; remaining customers passed tougher compliance inspections — field staff visits to customer data centers, contract verification and end-user interviews — to screen out shell companies that forward NVIDIA GPUs and servers into China. Sources told the Financial Times the move followed pressure from Washington to tighten legal compliance. NVIDIA did not immediately comment.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\",\n     \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Beijing has refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA's U.S.-approved AI processors (H200 GPUs), instead relying on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the compute shortfall — a policy that bars the China sales Washington cleared, with domestic supply 'apparently still not enough.'\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In December 2025, President Donald Trump reversed U.S. policy and allowed NVIDIA to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in China/Asia, a step that would have eased the region's AI-chip shortage.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all Chinese domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out, and Chinese buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use — indicating unserved AI compute demand in China amid constrained supply.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 9,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Sharon AI (counterparty disclosure, reported by SemiAnalysis)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Sharon AI disclosed\",\n    \"what\": \"total value of NVIDIA's backstop on Sharon's 72MW Australian AI factory (up to 40,000 GB300)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$4.88B; implied floor ~$2.33/hr/GPU\",\n    \"period\": \"six years\",\n    \"condition\": \"up to 40,000 GB300s\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\",\n    \"status_note\": \"announced June 2026\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"the below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\",\n     \"Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\",\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 10,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Firmus (announced June 29, 2026; reported by SemiAnalysis)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n    \"what\": \"a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster\",\n    \"how_much\": \"360MW\",\n    \"period\": \"announced June 29, 2026\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\",\n    \"status_note\": \"announced\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"the below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\",\n     \"Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\",\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 11,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n    \"what\": \"expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$25B-$30B\",\n    \"period\": \"over six years\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"the below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\",\n     \"Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\",\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 12,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\",\n    \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"the below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\",\n     \"Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\",\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 13,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"who\": \"The Information (prior reporting)\",\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\",\n    \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\",\n    \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\",\n    \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\",\n    \"status\": \"rumour\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-mechanism\",\n    \"summary\": \"On July 1, The Information reported that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young GPU cloud providers: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return NVIDIA receives a percentage of the providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have joined the program, and three executives with existing ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\",\n     \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-nvidia-official\",\n    \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model combining revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per NVIDIA's press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based recurring revenue stream.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\",\n     \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-sharon\",\n    \"summary\": \"Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-firmus\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus, another first participant, is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-openai-lease\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio; if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, the asset could cost as much as $500 billion. The $500 billion figure is the data center's potential build-out cost, not the size of any NVIDIA guarantee.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 14,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n   \"who\": \"the big four hyperscalers (2026 plans, summarised by the author); NVIDIA (2027 claim)\",\n   \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n    \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n    \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n    \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n    \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the article, which frames this as the current-year aggregate AI infrastructure capex plan of NVIDIA's largest customers.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year, i.e. calendar 2027, according to the article; this is NVIDIA's forward claim about its largest customers' spending, not an observed outlay.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 15,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-04\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\",\n   \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus, as reported by the author)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts\",\n    \"what\": \"NVIDIA revenue growth forecast\",\n    \"how_much\": \"82% this year; 41% next year\",\n    \"period\": \"this year and next year (fiscal or calendar both accepted, D233)\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027), as an extension of the roughly $650 billion they plan to spend this year.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for NVIDIA this year (calendar 2026) and 41% revenue growth next year (calendar 2027), according to the article.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 16,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n   \"who\": \"unnamed estimates\",\n   \"passage\": \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"unnamed estimates\",\n    \"what\": \"memory's share of data center spend\",\n    \"how_much\": \"about half\",\n    \"period\": \"2027\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across the company's upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems, according to 24/7 Wall St.; the report does not identify the source of the delay reports, name which Vera Rubin products are affected, or give a new timing.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center revenue up 92% to $75.2 billion, according to 24/7 Wall St. (the quarter is not specified in the item).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Broadcom guided fiscal Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year — a competitor's projected AI chip revenue in the market NVIDIA leads.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts a 27% compound annual growth rate for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators, as cited by 24/7 Wall St., with Broadcom's custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta taking share of the AI chip story.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Memory supplier Micron reported Q3 revenue rose 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, with CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointing to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements intended to lock in durability, according to 24/7 Wall St.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron is forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% for the next quarter, above the peak gross margin NVIDIA has ever achieved, 24/7 Wall St. reports — a memory supplier's margin outlook that reflects pricing power in NVIDIA's input chain.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Unnamed estimates cited by 24/7 Wall St. put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-8\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA carries $119 billion in supply commitments, which 24/7 Wall St. says hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools (the item gives no date or breakdown for the commitment).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 17,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"Goldman Sachs (as cited)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Goldman Sachs estimate\",\n    \"what\": \"annual AI infrastructure capex (chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$765B (2026); $1.6T (2031); ~$7.6T cumulative 2026-2031\",\n    \"period\": \"2026; 2031; 2026-2031\",\n    \"condition\": \"could reach\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported, for its third fiscal quarter of 2026, revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% revenue growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%), as recounted in the Berkshire Edge column by Allen Harris. Micron is presented as the AI memory supplier whose results are being lifted by AI data-center demand; the item gives no memory contract prices or NVIDIA-specific volumes or prices.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\",\n     \"That is why Nvidia’s success is spilling over into Micron.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In the Berkshire Edge column by Allen Harris, NVIDIA's latest reported metrics were compared with Micron's: NVIDIA's revenue growth was about 85% versus Micron's roughly 346%, NVIDIA's GAAP gross margin was 74.9% versus 84.6%, and NVIDIA's operating margin was roughly 66% versus 80.4%, with the column noting NVIDIA still earns more dollars. The item does not state the period of NVIDIA's figures; the comparison is drawn against Micron's third fiscal quarter of 2026.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited in the Berkshire Edge column by Allen Harris; that spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking. The item does not say when Goldman published the estimate.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported in the Berkshire Edge column by Allen Harris. The item gives no date for the announcement and no size of the price increases.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 18,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA (May 2026 quarterly filing)\",\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"what\": \"guarantees of customer data center leases, in exchange for rights to buy their shares\",\n    \"how_much\": \"an additional $3.5 billion\",\n    \"period\": \"quarter ended April 2026 (filed May)\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-mechanism\",\n    \"summary\": \"On July 1, The Information reported that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young GPU cloud providers: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return NVIDIA receives a percentage of the providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have joined the program, and three executives with existing ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\",\n     \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-nvidia-official\",\n    \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model combining revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per NVIDIA's press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based recurring revenue stream.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\",\n     \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-sharon\",\n    \"summary\": \"Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-firmus\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus, another first participant, is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-openai-lease\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio; if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, the asset could cost as much as $500 billion. The $500 billion figure is the data center's potential build-out cost, not the size of any NVIDIA guarantee.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 19,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"NVIDIA backstops datacenter leases for neoclouds (a second kind of obligation)\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"the below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\",\n     \"Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\",\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r6.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"point\\\":x[\\\"point\\\"],\\\"decisive\\\":x[\\\"decisive_item\\\"],\\\"kept\\\":[{\\\"ref\\\":e[\\\"ref\\\"],\\\"summary\\\":e[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"passages\\\":e[\\\"passages\\\"]} for e in x[\\\"kept_from_that_article\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False)) for x in p if 6 <= x[\\\"point\\\"] <= 16]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r6.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"point\\\":x[\\\"point\\\"],\\\"decisive\\\":x[\\\"decisive_item\\\"],\\\"kept\\\":[{\\\"ref\\\":e[\\\"ref\\\"],\\\"summary\\\":e[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"passages\\\":e[\\\"passages\\\"]} for e in x[\\\"kept_from_that_article\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False)) for x in p if 6 <= x[\\\"point\\\"] <= 16]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"point\": 6, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-14\", \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\", \"who\": \"China's government (stated by Tom's Hardware)\", \"passage\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Beijing\", \"what\": \"Chinese companies not allowed to buy NVIDIA H200\", \"status\": \"happened\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has reportedly created a new 'whitelist' of verified customers in Asia, cutting its number of authorized clients by more than half; remaining customers passed tougher compliance inspections — field staff visits to customer data centers, contract verification and end-user interviews — to screen out shell companies that forward NVIDIA GPUs and servers into China. Sources told the Financial Times the move followed pressure from Washington to tighten legal compliance. NVIDIA did not immediately comment.\", \"passages\": [\"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\", \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\", \"summary\": \"Beijing has refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA's U.S.-approved AI processors (H200 GPUs), instead relying on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the compute shortfall — a policy that bars the China sales Washington cleared, with domestic supply 'apparently still not enough.'\", \"passages\": [\"However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\", \"summary\": \"In December 2025, President Donald Trump reversed U.S. policy and allowed NVIDIA to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in China/Asia, a step that would have eased the region's AI-chip shortage.\", \"passages\": [\"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\", \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all Chinese domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out, and Chinese buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use — indicating unserved AI compute demand in China amid constrained supply.\", \"passages\": [\"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 7, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-14\", \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\", \"who\": \"an unnamed tech executive (to the Financial Times)\", \"passage\": \"all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"unnamed Chinese tech executive\", \"what\": \"China's domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out; buyers are considering less powerful chips\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has reportedly created a new 'whitelist' of verified customers in Asia, cutting its number of authorized clients by more than half; remaining customers passed tougher compliance inspections — field staff visits to customer data centers, contract verification and end-user interviews — to screen out shell companies that forward NVIDIA GPUs and servers into China. Sources told the Financial Times the move followed pressure from Washington to tighten legal compliance. NVIDIA did not immediately comment.\", \"passages\": [\"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\", \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\", \"summary\": \"Beijing has refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA's U.S.-approved AI processors (H200 GPUs), instead relying on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the compute shortfall — a policy that bars the China sales Washington cleared, with domestic supply 'apparently still not enough.'\", \"passages\": [\"However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\", \"summary\": \"In December 2025, President Donald Trump reversed U.S. policy and allowed NVIDIA to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in China/Asia, a step that would have eased the region's AI-chip shortage.\", \"passages\": [\"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\", \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all Chinese domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out, and Chinese buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use — indicating unserved AI compute demand in China amid constrained supply.\", \"passages\": [\"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 8, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-14\", \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\", \"who\": \"US President (as reported)\", \"passage\": \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"US President Trump\", \"what\": \"allowed H200 exports to select customers in China\", \"period\": \"December 2025\", \"status\": \"happened\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has reportedly created a new 'whitelist' of verified customers in Asia, cutting its number of authorized clients by more than half; remaining customers passed tougher compliance inspections — field staff visits to customer data centers, contract verification and end-user interviews — to screen out shell companies that forward NVIDIA GPUs and servers into China. Sources told the Financial Times the move followed pressure from Washington to tighten legal compliance. NVIDIA did not immediately comment.\", \"passages\": [\"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\", \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\", \"summary\": \"Beijing has refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA's U.S.-approved AI processors (H200 GPUs), instead relying on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the compute shortfall — a policy that bars the China sales Washington cleared, with domestic supply 'apparently still not enough.'\", \"passages\": [\"However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\", \"summary\": \"In December 2025, President Donald Trump reversed U.S. policy and allowed NVIDIA to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in China/Asia, a step that would have eased the region's AI-chip shortage.\", \"passages\": [\"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\", \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all Chinese domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out, and Chinese buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use — indicating unserved AI compute demand in China amid constrained supply.\", \"passages\": [\"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 9, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"Sharon AI (counterparty disclosure, reported by SemiAnalysis)\", \"passage\": \"Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Sharon AI disclosed\", \"what\": \"total value of NVIDIA's backstop on Sharon's 72MW Australian AI factory (up to 40,000 GB300)\", \"how_much\": \"$4.88B; implied floor ~$2.33/hr/GPU\", \"period\": \"six years\", \"condition\": \"up to 40,000 GB300s\", \"status\": \"happened\", \"status_note\": \"announced June 2026\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\", \"passages\": [\"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\", \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\", \"passages\": [\"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\", \"passages\": [\"the below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\", \"Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\", \"summary\": \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\", \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\", \"summary\": \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\", \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\", \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\", \"passages\": [\"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\", \"passages\": [\"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 10, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"Firmus (announced June 29, 2026; reported by SemiAnalysis)\", \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Firmus\", \"what\": \"a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster\", \"how_much\": \"360MW\", \"period\": \"announced June 29, 2026\", \"status\": \"happened\", \"status_note\": \"announced\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\", \"passages\": [\"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\", \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\", \"passages\": [\"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\", \"passages\": [\"the below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\", \"Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\", \"summary\": \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\", \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\", \"summary\": \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\", \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\", \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\", \"passages\": [\"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\", \"passages\": [\"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 11, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"Firmus\", \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Firmus\", \"what\": \"expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)\", \"how_much\": \"$25B-$30B\", \"period\": \"over six years\", \"status\": \"forecast\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\", \"passages\": [\"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\", \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\", \"passages\": [\"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\", \"passages\": [\"the below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\", \"Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\", \"summary\": \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\", \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\", \"summary\": \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\", \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\", \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\", \"passages\": [\"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\", \"passages\": [\"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 12, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\", \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\", \"status\": \"claim\", \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\", \"passages\": [\"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\", \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\", \"passages\": [\"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\", \"passages\": [\"the below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\", \"Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\", \"summary\": \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\", \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\", \"summary\": \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\", \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\", \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\", \"passages\": [\"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\", \"passages\": [\"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 13, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-01\", \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\", \"who\": \"The Information (prior reporting)\", \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\", \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\", \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\", \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\", \"status\": \"rumour\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-mechanism\", \"summary\": \"On July 1, The Information reported that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young GPU cloud providers: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return NVIDIA receives a percentage of the providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have joined the program, and three executives with existing ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\", \"passages\": [\"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\", \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-nvidia-official\", \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model combining revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per NVIDIA's press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based recurring revenue stream.\", \"passages\": [\"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\", \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-sharon\", \"summary\": \"Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\", \"passages\": [\"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-firmus\", \"summary\": \"Firmus, another first participant, is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\", \"passages\": [\"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-openai-lease\", \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio; if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, the asset could cost as much as $500 billion. The $500 billion figure is the data center's potential build-out cost, not the size of any NVIDIA guarantee.\", \"passages\": [\"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 14, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\", \"who\": \"the big four hyperscalers (2026 plans, summarised by the author); NVIDIA (2027 claim)\", \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\", \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\", \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\", \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\", \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c#e1\", \"summary\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the article, which frames this as the current-year aggregate AI infrastructure capex plan of NVIDIA's largest customers.\", \"passages\": [\"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c#e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year, i.e. calendar 2027, according to the article; this is NVIDIA's forward claim about its largest customers' spending, not an observed outlay.\", \"passages\": [\"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 15, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-04\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\", \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus, as reported by the author)\", \"passage\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Wall Street analysts\", \"what\": \"NVIDIA revenue growth forecast\", \"how_much\": \"82% this year; 41% next year\", \"period\": \"this year and next year (fiscal or calendar both accepted, D233)\", \"status\": \"forecast\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#e1\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\", \"passages\": [\"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027), as an extension of the roughly $650 billion they plan to spend this year.\", \"passages\": [\"Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#e3\", \"summary\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for NVIDIA this year (calendar 2026) and 41% revenue growth next year (calendar 2027), according to the article.\", \"passages\": [\"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 16, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\", \"who\": \"unnamed estimates\", \"passage\": \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"unnamed estimates\", \"what\": \"memory's share of data center spend\", \"how_much\": \"about half\", \"period\": \"2027\", \"status\": \"forecast\"}}, \"kept\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across the company's upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems, according to 24/7 Wall St.; the report does not identify the source of the delay reports, name which Vera Rubin products are affected, or give a new timing.\", \"passages\": [\"NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center revenue up 92% to $75.2 billion, according to 24/7 Wall St. (the quarter is not specified in the item).\", \"passages\": [\"Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Broadcom guided fiscal Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year — a competitor's projected AI chip revenue in the market NVIDIA leads.\", \"passages\": [\"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts a 27% compound annual growth rate for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators, as cited by 24/7 Wall St., with Broadcom's custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta taking share of the AI chip story.\", \"passages\": [\"Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-5\", \"summary\": \"Memory supplier Micron reported Q3 revenue rose 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, with CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointing to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements intended to lock in durability, according to 24/7 Wall St.\", \"passages\": [\"Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-6\", \"summary\": \"Micron is forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% for the next quarter, above the peak gross margin NVIDIA has ever achieved, 24/7 Wall St. reports — a memory supplier's margin outlook that reflects pricing power in NVIDIA's input chain.\", \"passages\": [\"Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-7\", \"summary\": \"Unnamed estimates cited by 24/7 Wall St. put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\", \"passages\": [\"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-8\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA carries $119 billion in supply commitments, which 24/7 Wall St. says hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools (the item gives no date or breakdown for the commitment).\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\"]}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r6.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"point\\\":x[\\\"point\\\"],\\\"decisive\\\":x[\\\"decisive_item\\\"],\\\"kept\\\":[[e[\\\"ref\\\"],e[\\\"summary\\\"]] for e in x[\\\"kept_from_that_article\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False)) for x in p if x[\\\"point\\\"] in (10,11)]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r6.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"point\\\":x[\\\"point\\\"],\\\"decisive\\\":x[\\\"decisive_item\\\"],\\\"kept\\\":[[e[\\\"ref\\\"],e[\\\"summary\\\"]] for e in x[\\\"kept_from_that_article\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False)) for x in p if x[\\\"point\\\"] in (10,11)]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"point\": 10, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"Firmus (announced June 29, 2026; reported by SemiAnalysis)\", \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Firmus\", \"what\": \"a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster\", \"how_much\": \"360MW\", \"period\": \"announced June 29, 2026\", \"status\": \"happened\", \"status_note\": \"announced\"}}, \"kept\": [[\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\", \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\", \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\", \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\", \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\", \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\", \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\", \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\", \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\", \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\"]]}\n{\"point\": 11, \"decisive\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"Firmus\", \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Firmus\", \"what\": \"expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)\", \"how_much\": \"$25B-$30B\", \"period\": \"over six years\", \"status\": \"forecast\"}}, \"kept\": [[\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev1\", \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself: under the backstop program NVIDIA gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. Backstop programs are typically six years, during which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\", \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has gone deeper than GPU-rental backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases for Neoclouds, adding a further layer of credit support.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev3\", \"SemiAnalysis presents an illustrative NVIDIA backstop pricing curve averaging about $2.36/hr over the six-year period, which it believes is at the lower end of the expected backstop range and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops. In its modeled short-tenor scenario the NVIDIA revenue-share take rate works out to about 18% on average over six years.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\", \"SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year NVIDIA backstop; Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Sharon's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to reaching more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\", \"Firmus's 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced June 29, 2026, is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop. Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop level will be shared with NVIDIA.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev6\", \"SemiAnalysis estimates that the broader market remains materially lower on NVIDIA shipment volumes and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 than its own supply-chain-based Accelerator Model tracking implies.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev7\", \"SemiAnalysis notes AMD has already been providing GPU backstops starting last year; per its June 2025 AMD article, AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal in which, in exchange for buying more AMD GPUs, AMD would stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of the capacity on long-term contracts for internal AMD software development if the Neocloud could not fully sell it.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev8\", \"SemiAnalysis forecasts annual AI capex — including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house it — will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and credit markets the main funding source.\"], [\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev9\", \"SemiAnalysis forecasts AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and even hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\"]]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"point\":1,\"extracted\":\"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e1\",\"why\":\"Micron said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028.\"}\n{\"point\":2,\"extracted\":\"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e2\",\"why\":\"New memory capacity is not expected until 2027 or later, leaving supply low for at least another year and a half.\"}\n{\"point\":3,\"extracted\":\"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e4\",\"why\":\"Micron guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion.\"}\n{\"point\":4,\"extracted\":\"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-6\",\"why\":\"Micron forecast an 86% adjusted gross margin for the next quarter.\"}\n{\"point\":5,\"extracted\":\"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e1\",\"why\":\"China reportedly plans to allow selected AI companies to buy limited numbers of H200 chips with approval.\"}\n{\"point\":6,\"extracted\":\"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\",\"why\":\"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA H200 processors.\"}\n{\"point\":7,\"extracted\":\"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\",\"why\":\"An executive said domestic AI-chip suppliers were sold out and buyers were considering less powerful chips.\"}\n{\"point\":8,\"extracted\":\"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\",\"why\":\"Trump allowed H200 exports to select customers in the region in December 2025.\"}\n{\"point\":9,\"extracted\":\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev4\",\"why\":\"Sharon disclosed a $4.88 billion total backstop for its Australian AI factory.\"}\n{\"point\":10,\"extracted\":\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\",\"why\":\"Firmus announced its 360 MW Batam cluster with an NVIDIA backstop.\"}\n{\"point\":11,\"extracted\":\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev5\",\"why\":\"Firmus expected $25 billion to $30 billion in customer revenue over six years.\"}\n{\"point\":12,\"extracted\":\"none\",\"why\":\"No kept item states that many neoclouds are negotiating NVIDIA backstops or that announcements are accelerating.\"}\n{\"point\":13,\"extracted\":\"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev-openai-lease\",\"why\":\"NVIDIA was reportedly negotiating a guarantee for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease.\"}\n{\"point\":14,\"extracted\":\"gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c#e1\",\"why\":\"The item states the big four hyperscalers planned around $650 billion in data-center spending in 2026; the 2027 figure appears separately in e2.\"}\n{\"point\":15,\"extracted\":\"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#e3\",\"why\":\"Analysts projected NVIDIA revenue growth of 82% this year and 41% next year.\"}\n{\"point\":16,\"extracted\":\"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-7\",\"why\":\"Estimates put memory at half of data-center spending in 2027.\"}\n{\"point\":17,\"extracted\":\"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#e3\",\"why\":\"Goldman Sachs estimated $765 billion in AI infrastructure spending in 2026 and $1.6 trillion in 2031.\"}\n{\"point\":18,\"extracted\":\"none\",\"why\":\"No kept item states NVIDIA added $3.5 billion in customer data-center lease guarantees in exchange for share purchase rights.\"}\n{\"point\":19,\"extracted\":\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#ev2\",\"why\":\"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA had started backstopping neocloud data-center leases.\"}"}}
